{
  "image_name": "2a3e4365d9d176601eb6240418967a44.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two linked ideas for improving zinc batteries, especially at low temperature. The upper part explains why Zn plating/stripping becomes problematic below 0 °C. As temperature drops, zinc deposition appears sluggish and uneven, leading to dendrites and electrically isolated “dead Zn.” The schematic then shows a coulombic-efficiency measurement strategy: first form a Zn reservoir on a substrate, then repeatedly strip and plate a fixed amount of Zn for many cycles, and finally strip the remaining Zn. This sequence is used to evaluate how reversibly zinc can be cycled.\n\nThe lower part compares three advanced cathode configurations paired with different electrolytes. One uses an inorganic cathode with a zinc salt/triflate-based electrolyte, another uses an organic cathode with mixed halide/perchlorate electrolyte, and the third uses a carbon cathode with ZnCl₂/LiCl aqueous electrolyte. In each case, charge and discharge are illustrated by ion movement between a Zn anode and the cathode side. The comparison suggests that cathode chemistry and electrolyte design are being tuned together to enable better Zn²⁺ storage and more stable battery operation under challenging conditions.",
